WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.700
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.110
- So while I was on this
road trip headed North,

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.220
we got into New England and everything

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.849
started to look like this.

00:00:07.849 --> 00:00:10.580
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.920
There was Fishing Towns,

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.360
there was loads of clam
chowder everywhere.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.320
There were cliffs and
boats and lighthouses.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.993
This village is so cool.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.750
But wait, this isn't a family
vlog that we're doing here.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.523
You saw the title, we're
talking about a border dispute.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.210
So yeah, while I'm on this
trip, I do what I always do,

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:33.960
I look at the map of where we're going

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.730
and I sort of start to explore

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.190
and being the person that I am,

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.040
my attention always drifts away

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.600
from the actual road trip
route and starts focusing

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.540
on this weird line up here.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.660
These are the lines we
use to draw countries.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.970
We call them Borders.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:51.210
And man, the number of hours
I've spent tracing these lines

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.570
on a map just looking
at them, following them,

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:57.250
looking for, nothing just looking,

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.610
seeing what shaped this
important imaginary line takes.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.440
Most of the time it's pretty predictable,

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.620
a river or a straight line
determined by some treaty.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.350
But as I traced this US Canada Border,

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.540
the border that frames the State of Maine,

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:12.570
I eventually started to
get into the ocean where

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:16.010
this border line on Google
Maps runs into a hard stop

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.920
right here off the Coast of Maine.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.000
When I see something like this,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.150
my curiosity wheels just start spinning

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.124
and I can't not look into it.

00:01:24.124 --> 00:01:27.124
(indistinct voices)

00:01:27.976 --> 00:01:29.370
- What is this? A freak out?

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.250
- And it turns out that this line

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.960
that looks like the end of something

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.850
is actually just the beginning.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.360
Two lines, two different claims.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.350
This is the story of a little rocky island

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.090
with a solitary lighthouse.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.310
It's the story of Canadian
and American fishermen

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.960
looking for lobsters and
competing with each other.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.270
It's the story of Puffins and Tourists

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.420
and Donald Trump's Foreign Policy.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:54.770
This is the story of the

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:58.173
one territorial border dispute
between the US and Canada.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.251
- [Expounder] This area has
never been properly surveyed.

00:02:02.251 --> 00:02:04.490
It's as far as who owns what.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.393
- [Man] We don't wanna do
this, we have no choice.

00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:11.910
(dramatic music)

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.480
- This is the longest Border in the world.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.100
And it's not really full of much drama.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.120
If you rewind a bit, you'll see that

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.030
this whole area was claimed
by the British Empire.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.260
There was no Canada, there
was no United States.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.270
But eventually the
British settlers down here

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.040
decided to rebel from the Empire

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.290
and start their own country.

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:31.720
The British conceded all of this land

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.440
to the new United States of America.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.590
Fast forward 100 years and
you now have the country

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:38.570
of Canada and the border
that we know today begins

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.210
to take shape without too much conflict.

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.740
But over here in the ocean,
there were some problems.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.800
The treaty that the
British had signed with

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.870
the Newly Independent
Americans had said that

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.790
the United States could claim,

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.380
any territory that was South of this river

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.600
and that was within 20
leagues of its coast.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.340
There was this one
island over here called,

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.450
Machias Seal Island, which
was well within the claim

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.410
of the United States based on this treaty.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:07.080
But the British up here, which
were soon to be Canadians,

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.150
referenced an even earlier
document that said that

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.250
they owned any island that
was within six leagues

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:14.090
of their coast.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.500
So if you look at that island,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.920
you can see that there are
some overlapping claims.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:22.690
Both of these documents
gave this one island to

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.270
both of these countries.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:28.090
These conflicting treaties
caused all sorts of Border issues

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.200
in the ocean, up in New
England off the Coast of Maine.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.530
But no one really cared because there was

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.117
nothing valuable about
this piece of ocean.

00:03:36.117 --> 00:03:39.565
Not unless you fast forward
100 years to the 1970s.

00:03:39.565 --> 00:03:42.148
(upbeat music)

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:46.730
So it's the 1970s and the
United States and Canada

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.890
are realizing that this piece of ocean

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:50.420
is actually pretty valuable because it has

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.913
mineral deposits that
they want to extract.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:56.510
So they take their dispute

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.620
to the International Court of Justice,

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.540
which is in charge of
working out border disputes,

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.930
especially in the ocean.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.560
They both submit their claims

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.960
and over the next like five years

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.160
the International Court of Justice does

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:11.470
potentially the most
boring work in the world,

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:15.130
looking at so many measurements
and so many different charts

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:17.410
and maps and treaties to determine

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:18.680
where this border should be.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:21.710
And by the mid 1980s, they settled on

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:25.263
a clean, nice, border
that looks like this.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.053
Until it got right here.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.040
This is the area surrounding
Machias Seal Island,

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.610
a tiny rocky island that
both countries claim.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.220
There was no known minerals here.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.340
There was nothing valuable.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.910
All there was was this old lighthouse

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.000
that the British had built in the 1800s

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.360
and that the Canadians inherited.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.203
So the borders here
continued to look like this.

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.030
This is called the Grey Zone and it is the

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.690
one territorial border dispute between

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:56.323
the United States and Canada.

00:04:59.503 --> 00:05:02.140
Wait, wait, wait, pause,
look at this clip,

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.000
I did not shoot this clip myself.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.350
Didn't go into the middle of the ocean

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.320
to get this random clip.

